the main idea upon the european semester  is to ensure  that a series of economic targets and  growth tension measures are implemented  by the member states  in real terms what that means is that  this is a tool  to create jobs to promote sustainable  growth to get europe back to economic  accessibility and recovery  and these are the key priorities  actually of the irish presidency so this  is why  it's quite a natural fit for the irish  presence to ensure that the european  semester  is run as smoothly and as effectively as  possible so we meet the successful  goal of adopting the current country  pacific recommendations  uh by by june it is in fact a governance  structure to ensure that  the targets economic targets that have  been set up by the european council are  achieved  by the member states and the eu as a  whole by the 2020 deadline that we've  set ourselves  and it does this through first of all  starting with the annual growth survey  that's prepared by the commission in  november and then through  a process of consultation and  orientations given by the echo finn and  ebsco council and by the european  council and member states prepare  national reform programmes  and as on the basis of that then the  commission will prepare country pacific  recommendations  that give policy guidance to member  states on how to achieve these economic  targets and they in turn feed into the  preparation of their national budgets  each year in a legal sense it's not  binding  but certainly in a political sense it's  binding there are consequences if the  member states fail to implement the csrs  and there is an intense period of  monitoring and peer review within the  council to ensure that member states  implement the csrs  and but there's also mechanisms  available to the commission  and to give policy warnings if the cso  is not implemented within a certain  period of time  and also there's possibility of  sanctions or incentives  in this time where we're dealing with an  economic crisis we're trying to find  ways to  enhance growth and create jobs and the  main thing for the european  semester to do is to implement the  targets we set ourselves to make sure  that we create jobs and create growth  and they do this through a series of  events  and that not only monitor the  implementation of the  of the recommendations given to member  states and would also develop these  recommendations uh to ensure that  there's economic convergence among the  member states and that the member states  put in place um  key growth enhancing measures to create  jobs and growth within the member states  themselves it's quite easy for member  states to blame brussels  and but at the at the end of it it's all  about making sure that the  policies economically of each member  state is sustainable  and that that economic policy then is  sustainable into the future to create  jobs  sustainable jobs and sustainable growth  and so in the end of the day i think the  european union is very much in  the business of trying to help each  member state and not trying to punish  each member state  and without what's good for the member  states is good for the european union as  a whole which is good for the citizens  in terms of jobs  and growth
 

